# Everything a running bot writes. The journal names characters, and a character
# name plus this repository's doctrine files is enough to identify an account on a
# shared server. Same rule as the harness: a running fleet's output never lands in
# git.
/var/
/substrate/
*.log
*.ndjson

# A doctrine may name characters. The examples in doctrines/ deliberately do not,
# and anything local that does belongs beside them under this name.
/doctrines/local/
/doctrines/*.local.jsonc

# Per-machine agent configuration. This IS excluded by a global gitignore on the
# machine this repository was written on — which is exactly why it is repeated here.
# A public repository must not depend on a collaborator having the same global file,
# and the failure mode of assuming they do is that their local settings arrive in a
# pull request.
.claude/settings.local.json

node_modules/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Operator scripts. A restart command is an OPERATION, and the arguments one carries name
# accounts on a shared server — so the script that performs it belongs on the machine that
# owns the roster, next to the local doctrines, and not in a public repository. The tooling
# it drives is all committed; this is the one line that says which fleet to point it at.
/restart_all.ps1
/restart_all.local.ps1

# This machine's answers for the CLI: which doctrine, which fleet, where the broker is.
# `fleet` is a roster name and `doctrine` points under doctrines/local/, and dum-guard
# refuses both — same rule as the harness's roster files. dum.local.example.json is the
# committed shape.
/dum.local.json
